window.__hvBdRegister("livy-1", {"key":"livy-1","sections":[{"i":0,"j":9,"loc":"Book I, Preface and chs. 1–3","title":"Preface and the Trojan origins of Latium","summary":"Livy doubts whether his history will be worth the labour, but values the record of Rome as a monument of examples to imitate and avoid, and asks readers to watch how morals rose and then decayed down to his own age. He declines to affirm or refute the legends of Rome's founding. The narrative begins with Aeneas, spared at Troy, who lands at Laurentum, allies with King Latinus, marries Lavinia and founds Lavinium; after wars with Turnus and Mezentius he dies by the Numicus. Ascanius founds Alba Longa, and a line of Silvian kings ends with Amulius usurping his brother Numitor's throne and making Numitor's daughter Rhea Silvia a Vestal.","ideas":["History as a store of examples for private and public life","Decline of discipline and morals as the reader's chief concern","Aeneas unites Trojans and Aborigines under the name of Latins","Amulius's usurpation sets up the birth of the founders"],"terms":[{"t":"Jupiter Indiges","d":"The name under which the dead Aeneas was worshipped by the Numicus."},{"t":"Alba Longa","d":"City founded by Ascanius below the Alban Mount, seat of the Silvian kings."}]},{"i":10,"j":15,"loc":"Book I, chs. 4–8","title":"Romulus and Remus and the founding of Rome","summary":"Rhea Silvia bears twins and names Mars as their father; Amulius imprisons her and orders the boys thrown into the Tiber, but they are left at the floodwater's edge, suckled by a she-wolf and raised by the shepherd Faustulus and Larentia. Grown into leaders of young shepherds, they are recognised by Numitor after Remus's capture, kill Amulius and restore their grandfather. Quarrelling over augury about who should name and rule the new city, the brothers come to blows and Remus is killed. Romulus fortifies the Palatine, adopts the Greek rite of Hercules from Evander's story of Cacus, takes twelve lictors, opens an asylum for refugees and appoints a hundred senators called Fathers.","ideas":["Exposure of the twins and the she-wolf","Rival auguries of six and twelve vultures; death of Remus","Hercules slays Cacus and the cult of the Greatest Altar begins","Asylum and a senate of a hundred Fathers"],"terms":[{"t":"Lupercalia","d":"Festival on the Palatine said to come from Evander, in which youths ran naked in honour of Pan."},{"t":"patres (Fathers)","d":"Romulus's hundred senators, whose descendants were called patricians."},{"t":"lictors","d":"Attendants marking a magistrate's authority; Romulus took twelve, perhaps on an Etruscan model."}]},{"i":16,"j":26,"loc":"Book I, chs. 9–15","title":"Rape of the Sabine women and Romulus's wars","summary":"Refused intermarriage by its neighbours, Rome invites them to the Consualia and seizes their daughters; Romulus consoles the women with promises of citizenship and children. He defeats Caenina, killing its king and dedicating the first spoils to Jupiter Feretrius, then Antemnae and Crustumium. The Sabines under Titus Tatius take the citadel through Tarpeia's treachery and drive the Romans back until Romulus vows a temple to Jupiter the Stayer. The Sabine women rush between the armies and bring about peace and union of the two peoples; Tatius later dies at Lavinium, and Romulus defeats Fidenae and Veii.","ideas":["The women's intervention turns war into a single people","Tarpeia betrays the citadel and is crushed by shields","Thirty curiae named after the Sabine women","Romulus's reign ends with Rome strong enough for forty years of peace"],"terms":[{"t":"spolia opima (spoils of honour)","d":"Arms taken from an enemy king or commander killed in battle, dedicated to Jupiter Feretrius."},{"t":"Quirites","d":"Name given to the united citizens, from the Sabine town of Cures."},{"t":"curiae","d":"The thirty wards into which Romulus divided the people."}]},{"i":27,"j":33,"loc":"Book I, chs. 16–21","title":"Romulus vanishes and Numa's reign of peace","summary":"During a muster Romulus disappears in a storm; the people hail him as a god, though Livy notes a rumour that the senators killed him, and Proculus Julius reports a vision of Romulus promising Rome rule of the world. After a year of interregnum the people, with the Fathers' ratification, choose the Sabine Numa Pompilius, whose kingship is confirmed by augury; Livy rejects the story that Pythagoras taught him. Numa builds the temple of Janus, claims the guidance of the goddess Egeria, reforms the calendar, and founds priesthoods: flamens, Vestals, Salii and a pontifex. Religious fear and good faith replace the fear of laws, and Numa keeps peace for forty-three years.","ideas":["Apotheosis of Romulus and the rumour of murder","Origin of the interregnum and senatorial ratification of elections","Religion as a means to tame a warlike people","Romulus founded Rome by war, Numa by peace"],"terms":[{"t":"interregnum","d":"Rotating five-day rule by senators between kings."},{"t":"pontifex","d":"Priest given written rules for all rites, to whom the commons could turn on religious law."},{"t":"Janus","d":"Temple whose doors open in war and are closed when all neighbours are at peace."}]},{"i":34,"j":41,"loc":"Book I, chs. 22–26","title":"Tullus Hostilius, Alba, and the Horatii","summary":"The warlike Tullus Hostilius engineers a pretext for war with Alba by demanding redress first. The Alban dictator Mettius Fufetius warns of the Etruscan threat and proposes deciding dominion without a general battle; after a fetial treaty with a sacrificed pig, three Roman Horatii fight three Alban Curiatii. Two Romans fall, but the surviving Horatius feigns flight, separates his wounded pursuers and kills all three, winning Rome sovereignty over Alba. On his return he kills his sister for mourning a Curiatius; condemned for treason by duumvirs, he appeals to the people and is acquitted on his father's plea, with an expiatory rite of passing under the Sister's Beam.","ideas":["War declared under forms that cast guilt on the other side","The combat of the triplets decides the fate of two nations","Horatius kills his sister for mourning an enemy","First appeal to the people (provocatio) saves the hero"],"terms":[{"t":"fetial","d":"Priest who made treaties and sought redress in the name of the Roman People."},{"t":"pater patratus","d":"The fetial appointed to swear the oath that solemnises a treaty."},{"t":"perduellio (treason)","d":"The charge on which the duumvirs condemned Horatius."}]},{"i":42,"j":50,"loc":"Book I, chs. 27–31","title":"Mettius's treachery and the destruction of Alba","summary":"Mettius Fufetius secretly incites Fidenae and Veii to war and, in the battle, withdraws his Albans to the hills to wait on fortune. Tullus covers the desertion by announcing it as his own order, routs the enemy, and then at an assembly has Mettius torn apart by two chariots, a punishment Livy calls unique in Rome. Alba is demolished, its temples spared, and its people moved to Rome, doubling the citizens and adding the Caelian Hill and Alban noble families to the senate. Tullus defeats the Sabines, but after prodigies and a plague he turns to superstition, botches a rite of Numa's to Jupiter Elicius, and is killed by a thunderbolt after thirty-two years.","ideas":["A divided heart punished by a body torn in two","The silent grief of Alba's evacuation","Rome grows by absorbing conquered peoples","Neglect and misuse of religion bring Tullus's end"],"terms":[{"t":"Curia Hostilia","d":"Senate-house built by Tullus for the enlarged senate."},{"t":"prodigy","d":"A portent, here a rain of stones on the Alban Mount, answered with a nine days' rite."}]},{"i":51,"j":55,"loc":"Book I, chs. 32–34","title":"Ancus Marcius and the arrival of Lucumo","summary":"Numa's grandson Ancus Marcius restores the public rites but, provoked by Latin raids, gives Rome a ceremonial of war: the fetial procedure of demanding redress and declaring war by casting a spear into enemy territory, which Livy sets out in full. Ancus takes Politorium, Medullia and other Latin towns, settles their people on the Aventine, joins the Janiculum to the city with the first bridge over the Tiber, digs the Quirites' Ditch, builds a prison and founds Ostia. Meanwhile Lucumo, son of the Corinthian exile Demaratus, moves from Tarquinii to Rome at the urging of his ambitious wife Tanaquil. An eagle lifting and replacing his cap is read by Tanaquil as an omen of greatness; as Lucius Tarquinius Priscus he becomes the king's trusted friend and his children's guardian.","ideas":["Ancus joins Numa's piety to Romulus's warlikeness","Formal fetial declaration of war","Rome open to talent from outside","Tanaquil's reading of the eagle omen"],"terms":[{"t":"rerum repetitio (demand for redress)","d":"The fetial's formal claim, after thirty-three days of which war may be declared."},{"t":"Ostia","d":"Colony at the Tiber's mouth founded by Ancus, with salt-works nearby."}]},{"i":56,"j":64,"loc":"Book I, chs. 35–41","title":"Tarquinius Priscus: wars, works, and murder","summary":"After Ancus's death Tarquinius sends the king's sons hunting and becomes the first to canvass for the kingship, arguing that foreigners had ruled before. He adds a hundred senators, lays out the Circus Maximus, defeats the Sabines with doubled cavalry and a burning bridge, subdues the Ancient Latins, and begins walls, sewers and the Capitoline temple. The augur Attus Navius, opposing his change to the cavalry, cuts a whetstone with a razor, after which nothing is done without auspices. A flame plays harmlessly around the sleeping child Servius Tullius, whom Tanaquil raises as a future protector; the sons of Ancus have Tarquinius killed with an axe, and Tanaquil conceals his death while Servius takes up royal duties.","ideas":["First canvass for royal office","Attus Navius's miracle secures the authority of augury","The flaming head of Servius as a sign of destiny","Tanaquil manages the succession from the palace window"],"terms":[{"t":"auspices","d":"Signs from birds that, after Attus Navius, had to be taken before any public act."},{"t":"patres minorum gentium","d":"The hundred senators of the 'lesser families' added by Tarquinius."}]},{"i":65,"j":71,"loc":"Book I, chs. 42–48","title":"Servius Tullius: census, classes, and Tullia's crime","summary":"Servius marries his daughters to Tarquinius's sons, wins a war with Veii, and institutes the census, dividing citizens into five classes and centuries by wealth with matching arms, so that voting power rests with the leading citizens. He closes the first lustrum with eighty thousand registered, adds the Quirinal, Viminal and Esquiline, and extends the pomerium. He persuades the Latins to build a joint temple of Diana in Rome, and a Roman priest secures a prophetic heifer's sacrifice by trickery. The ambitious Tullia, after the deaths of her husband and sister, marries Lucius Tarquinius and drives him to seize the throne; he throws Servius down the senate steps, Servius is murdered, and Tullia drives her carriage over her father's body.","ideas":["Census distributes burdens and votes by wealth","Diana's shrine as a Latin admission of Rome's primacy","Tullia's goading of Tarquinius to usurpation","The Street of Crime; just kingship ends with Servius"],"terms":[{"t":"census","d":"Registration of citizens by property, the basis of classes, centuries and tribute."},{"t":"lustrum","d":"Purificatory sacrifice that closed the census."},{"t":"pomerium","d":"Consecrated strip on both sides of the city wall, kept free of building and tillage."}]},{"i":72,"j":80,"loc":"Book I, chs. 49–56","title":"Tarquinius Superbus: tyranny, Turnus, and Gabii","summary":"Lucius Tarquinius, called Superbus, refuses Servius burial, kills senators, takes a bodyguard, judges capital cases alone and ignores the senate, while binding the Latin nobles to him. When Turnus Herdonius denounces him at the Ferentine council, Tarquinius plants swords in his lodging and has him drowned as a conspirator, then renews the Latin treaty on Roman terms. He takes Suessa Pometia from the Volsci and wins Gabii by fraud: his son Sextus poses as a refugee, becomes the Gabine commander and, after his father silently strikes off the tallest poppies, destroys the city's leading men. Tarquinius builds the Capitoline temple, where Terminus refuses to move and a human head is found, and forces the plebs to labour on the circus and the Great Sewer.","ideas":["Rule by fear without senate or people","The framed conspiracy of Turnus Herdonius","The poppy-heads as a wordless order","Omens of Terminus and the head promise lasting empire"],"terms":[{"t":"Superbus","d":"'The Proud', the surname Tarquinius earned by his conduct."},{"t":"Cloaca Maxima (Great Sewer)","d":"Underground drain built with plebeian forced labour."}]},{"i":81,"j":86,"loc":"Book I, chs. 56–60","title":"Brutus, Lucretia, and the expulsion of the kings","summary":"Alarmed by a snake in the palace, Tarquinius sends two sons to Delphi with his nephew Lucius Junius Brutus, who feigns dullness to survive; told that the next ruler will be the first to kiss his mother, Brutus falls and kisses the earth. During the siege of Ardea a wager on wives shows Lucretia at her wool while the princes' wives feast, and Sextus Tarquinius returns to rape her under threat of disgrace. Lucretia tells her father and husband, names Sextus, and kills herself, saying that the mind, not the body, sins but that no unchaste woman shall live by her example. Brutus swears on the knife to drive out the kings, rouses Collatia and Rome with a speech on the tyrant's crimes, wins over the army, and the Tarquins are exiled; Brutus and Collatinus become the first consuls after 244 years of kings.","ideas":["Brutus's feigned stupidity and the Delphic oracle","Lucretia's chastity contest, rape, and suicide","The oath on the bloodied knife","Kingship abolished; two consuls elected"],"terms":[{"t":"Brutus","d":"'Dullard', the surname Junius accepted as a mask for his purpose."},{"t":"consuls","d":"The two annual magistrates chosen in the centuriate assembly after the kings."}]},{"i":87,"j":100,"loc":"Summary of Book I","title":"Ancient summaries of Book One","summary":"Two short ancient summaries recapitulate the book reign by reign. They list Aeneas and the Alban kings, Romulus's founding and wars, Numa's rites, Tullus's war with Alba and the triplets, Ancus's conquests and Ostia, and Tarquinius Priscus's wall and sewers. They note Servius's census of eighty thousand and Diana's temple, then Superbus's usurpation, the fraud at Gabii, the Delphic oracle and Brutus's kiss of the earth, Lucretia's death and the first consuls. One version gives the kings 255 years, differing from the 244 in Livy's text.","ideas":["Reign-by-reign recap of Book I","Attus Navius and the whetstone recalled","Different total for the regal period"],"terms":[]},{"i":101,"j":106,"loc":"Book II, chs. 1–7","title":"First year of liberty and Brutus's sons","summary":"Livy argues that liberty came at the right time, once the people had matured, and that it lay in annual office rather than reduced power. Brutus binds the people by oath to suffer no king, fills the senate to three hundred, and persuades his colleague Collatinus to resign because of the hated Tarquinian name; Publius Valerius replaces him. Young nobles who miss royal licence plot with Tarquin's envoys, among them Brutus's own sons; a slave betrays them, the royal goods are given to the plebs, and Brutus watches his sons scourged and beheaded. Tarquinius raises Veii and Tarquinii, and in the battle Brutus and Arruns Tarquinius kill each other; a voice from the Arsian forest declares Rome the victor.","ideas":["Liberty defined by annual magistracy and law","The young nobles' complaint that law has no ears","A father as consul executes his own sons","Brutus dies in single combat with Arruns"],"terms":[{"t":"rex sacrorum (king of sacrifices)","d":"Priest appointed to perform the kings' rites, kept under the pontifex."},{"t":"vindicta","d":"Form of manumission said to derive from the informer Vindicius."},{"t":"Campus Martius","d":"Tarquin land consecrated to Mars after the expulsion."}]},{"i":107,"j":113,"loc":"Book II, chs. 7–11","title":"Publicola, Porsinna, and Horatius at the bridge","summary":"Suspected of aiming at kingship, Valerius lowers his fasces before the people and moves his house below the Velia; his laws on appeal to the people and against would-be kings earn him the name Publicola. Horatius Pulvillus dedicates the Capitoline temple despite false news of his son's death. Lars Porsinna of Clusium attacks Rome for the Tarquins, and the senate wins the plebs over with corn purchases, a state salt monopoly and tax relief. Horatius Cocles holds the bridge alone, first with Larcius and Herminius, until it is cut down, then swims the Tiber in his armour; Porsinna turns to blockade, and Valerius ambushes his raiders.","ideas":["The consul's fasces lowered to the people's majesty","Law of appeal earns the name Publicola","Concessions to the plebs keep harmony in the siege","Horatius Cocles defends the bridge"],"terms":[{"t":"Publicola","d":"'The People's Friend', surname of Publius Valerius."},{"t":"provocatio (appeal)","d":"Right to appeal from a magistrate to the people, secured by Valerius's law."},{"t":"fasces","d":"Rods carried by lictors as emblems of a magistrate's power."}]},{"i":114,"j":119,"loc":"Book II, chs. 12–15","title":"Mucius Scaevola, Cloelia, and peace with Porsinna","summary":"Gaius Mucius enters Porsinna's camp to kill him but stabs the secretary by mistake; captured, he thrusts his right hand into the altar fire and claims three hundred youths are sworn to follow. The shaken king releases him and offers peace, recovering Veientine land and taking hostages. The hostage Cloelia leads the girls across the Tiber; Porsinna demands her return, then honours her and lets her free half the hostages, and Rome sets up her equestrian statue. Livy explains the auction phrase 'goods of King Porsinna', recounts Arruns's defeat at Aricia, and ends with the senate's embassy telling Porsinna that Rome would sooner open its gates to enemies than to kings, so that he abandons the Tarquins.","ideas":["'To do and to endure' as the Roman way","Courage of the hostage Cloelia","Etruscan refugees settle the Vicus Tuscus","Final refusal to restore the Tarquins"],"terms":[{"t":"Scaevola","d":"'Left-handed', the name Mucius gained after losing his right hand."},{"t":"obsides (hostages)","d":"Pledges given to Porsinna for withdrawal of his garrison from Janiculum."}]},{"i":120,"j":127,"loc":"Book II, chs. 16–21","title":"First dictator and the battle of Lake Regillus","summary":"Attius Clausus migrates from the Sabines with his clients and becomes Appius Claudius; Publicola dies so poor that the state pays his funeral. The Romans destroy the Aurunci and Pometia without quarter. Facing the Sabines and thirty Latin cities roused by Octavius Mamilius, Rome names its first dictator, probably Titus Largius, whose unappealable power awes the plebs. At Lake Regillus the dictator Postumius defeats the Latins and the Tarquins in a battle of leaders: Superbus is wounded, Marcus Valerius and Mamilius killed, and the knights fight on foot; Livy notes the confusion of dates. Tarquinius dies at Cumae, and with that the nobles begin to oppress the plebs.","ideas":["Origins of the Claudian family","Dictatorship created against external and internal danger","Lake Regillus fought by commanders in person","Tarquin's death removes the restraint on the nobles"],"terms":[{"t":"dictator","d":"Single supreme magistrate with no colleague and no appeal from his authority."},{"t":"magister equitum (master of the horse)","d":"The dictator's second-in-command."}]},{"i":128,"j":134,"loc":"Book II, chs. 22–27","title":"The debt crisis and Appius versus Servilius","summary":"After a Volscian threat the grateful senate frees Latin captives. At home the plebs rage over debt bondage, set off when an old centurion, ruined by war, taxes and usury and scourged by his creditor, shows his scars and his back in the Forum. Appius Claudius wants force while Servilius prefers conciliation; when the Volsci attack, Servilius forbids holding citizens in bonds so they can enlist, and the debtors fight well as he takes the Volscian camp and Suessa Pometia. Quick victories over the Sabines and Aurunci follow, but Appius then enforces debt judgments with full rigour, Servilius wavers and is hated by both sides, and the people humiliate the consuls by giving the temple dedication to the centurion Laetorius.","ideas":["Freedom more secure abroad than at home for indebted soldiers","Servilius's edict buys enlistment with a promise","Appius's severity and the broken promise","Debtors turn violent against creditors"],"terms":[{"t":"nexi (bound over for debt)","d":"Debtors delivered to creditors' custody for non-payment."}]},{"i":135,"j":143,"loc":"Book II, chs. 28–33","title":"Secession of the plebs and the first tribunes","summary":"The plebs meet at night on the hills and refuse the levy; the senate debates Verginius's limited relief, Largius's general relief, and Appius's call for a dictator, and chooses the mild Manius Valerius. His edict fills ten legions, and Rome beats the Volsci, the Sabines and the Aequi, but when the senate refuses relief for debtors Valerius resigns in protest. The senate keeps the army in the field under its oath, and the soldiers, on Sicinius's advice, secede to the Sacred Mount. Agrippa Menenius wins them back with the fable of the limbs that starved the belly, and the plebs gain inviolable tribunes of their own; in the same year Gnaeus Marcius wins the name Coriolanus by taking Corioli, and Menenius dies poor.","ideas":["The army's oath used to keep the plebs in arms","Secession to the Sacred Mount without violence","The fable of the belly and the members","Creation of the tribunes of the people"],"terms":[{"t":"secessio (secession)","d":"Collective withdrawal of the plebs from the city."},{"t":"tribunus plebis (tribune of the people)","d":"Inviolable plebeian magistrate with the right to aid the people against the consuls."},{"t":"sacrosanctitas (inviolability)","d":"Sacred protection of the tribunes' persons, enacted on the Sacred Mount."}]},{"i":144,"j":153,"loc":"Book II, chs. 34–41","title":"Coriolanus's exile, march, and Spurius Cassius","summary":"After a famine, Coriolanus proposes selling Sicilian corn cheaply only if the plebs give up their tribunes; the tribunes summon him to trial, and condemned in absence he goes to the Volscian leader Attius Tullius. After Titus Latinius's dream forces a repetition of the Great Games, Tullius tricks the consuls into expelling the visiting Volsci and uses the insult to raise the whole nation. Coriolanus takes town after town and camps five miles from Rome, rejecting envoys and priests, until his mother Veturia, his wife Volumnia and his children come and Veturia's reproach makes him withdraw; the temple of Fortuna Muliebris marks the women's deed. The section ends with the Volsci and Aequi destroying each other in a quarrel and with Spurius Cassius's first agrarian proposal, opposed by Verginius as a road to kingship, and his condemnation and execution.","ideas":["Coriolanus would trade corn for the end of the tribunate","Attius Tullius's ruse turns festival insult into war","Veturia's appeal saves the city","First agrarian law and the fear of a would-be king"],"terms":[{"t":"Fortuna Muliebris","d":"Temple to Women's Fortune built to commemorate the women's embassy."},{"t":"agrarian law","d":"Proposal to distribute public land, first made by Spurius Cassius."}]},{"i":154,"j":160,"loc":"Book II 42–47","title":"The Fabii and the great battle against Veii","summary":"After Cassius's fall the land-law keeps returning, and the patricians keep the consulship in the Fabian family while the tribunes are blocked. Caeso Fabius's army refuses to pursue the beaten Aequi out of hatred for him, and Appius Claudius advises the senate to break tribunician obstruction by winning over some tribunes against a colleague. Against Veii and the Etruscans, who count on Roman discord, the consuls Marcus Fabius and Gnaeus Manlius hold back until the jeering enemy goads the army into swearing to return victorious. In the battle Quintus Fabius and the consul Manlius are killed, but the Romans win, and Marcus Fabius refuses a triumph and has the wounded nursed in patrician homes, regaining the people's goodwill for his clan.","ideas":["The army deliberately throws away a victory to spite its hated consul","Appius's plan: divide the tribunes and use one against the rest","The soldiers' oath to return victorious, sworn before the battle with Veii","Fabius's refusal of a triumph increases his glory"],"terms":[{"t":"lex agraria (land-law)","d":"The tribunes' recurring proposal to distribute public land to the plebs, resisted by the patricians who held it."},{"t":"tribunician veto","d":"A single tribune could block his colleagues, which the patricians learn to exploit."}]},{"i":161,"j":171,"loc":"Book II 48–53","title":"The Fabii fall at the Cremera","summary":"Caeso Fabius urges the senate to give conquered land to the plebs, but is rebuffed. Since the war with Veii is endless raiding, the Fabian clan offers to fight it at its own cost, and 306 Fabii march out by the Unlucky Way and build a fort on the Cremera. After many successes they are lured by scattered flocks into an ambush and killed to a man, with one boy surviving to carry on the family. The Etruscans then seize Janiculum and threaten Rome until they are beaten; the tribunes prosecute Menenius, who dies after his conviction, while Servilius defies his accusers and is acquitted, and a combined Veientine and Sabine force is crushed by Valerius.","ideas":["A single clan undertakes a state war as a private feud","The Fabii grow overconfident and are destroyed by a feigned flight and ambush","Etruscans on Janiculum and food shortage in Rome","Tribunes use trials against former consuls"],"terms":[{"t":"gens (clan)","d":"The Fabian family, 306 patricians of one blood, acts as an army on its own."}]},{"i":172,"j":181,"loc":"Book II 54–60","title":"Volero's law and the decimation under Appius","summary":"After a forty-year truce with Veii, the tribune Genucius prosecutes the former consuls, but on the day of trial he is found dead at home, and the cowed tribunes allow a levy. Volero Publilius, a former centurion, resists being enrolled as a common soldier, beats off the lictors and drives the consuls from the Forum. Elected tribune, he proposes that plebeian magistrates be chosen in the tribal assembly; after a violent clash between the tribune Laetorius and the consul Appius Claudius, Quinctius calms the crowd and the senate lets the law pass. In the field Appius's army deliberately fails him against the Volsci, and he scourges and beheads the guilty and executes every tenth man, while Quinctius's gentle command against the Aequi brings rich booty and goodwill.","ideas":["The murder of the tribune Genucius shows the weakness of tribunician sanctity","Volero's resistance to the consuls' lictors","Tribunes elected by the tribal assembly, removing patrician client votes","Appius's harshness against Quinctius's mildness: a tyrant and a parent to their armies"],"terms":[{"t":"comitia tributa (tribal assembly)","d":"An assembly voting by tribes, now used to elect plebeian magistrates."},{"t":"decimation","d":"Execution of every tenth soldier chosen by lot, inflicted by Appius on his army."}]},{"i":182,"j":189,"loc":"Book II 61–65; Summary of Book II","title":"Trial of Appius, wars, and Antium taken","summary":"Appius Claudius, the fiercest opponent of the land-law, is prosecuted before the people but defends himself as arrogantly as ever; the trial is adjourned and he dies of illness, and the plebs attend his funeral in crowds. Campaigns against the Aequi and Sabines are inconclusive, a storm halting one assault. Under later consuls the Volsci are beaten, and Quinctius uses mounted trumpeters to keep the enemy awake all night, then wins on the hills and takes Antium. The Summary of Book II then lists the book's events from Brutus's oath to Appius's decimation.","ideas":["Appius's unbending defiance overawes his accusers","A sudden storm stops the attack on the Aequian camp, as if a god defended it","Quinctius's night stratagem with trumpeters on horseback","Capture of Antium ends the book"],"terms":[]},{"i":190,"j":203,"loc":"Book III 1–7","title":"Antium colony, Aequian wars, and the plague","summary":"Quintus Fabius, the survivor of the Cremera, settles the land dispute by proposing a colony at Antium, though few Romans enroll. The Aequi break faith, are beaten by Fabius, then raid toward Rome; later the consul Furius is besieged in his camp, and the senate gives Postumius emergency powers, while Quinctius relieves the army with Latin and Hernican troops. Then a pestilence strikes Rome, killing both consuls and many leading men, and the senate can only tell the Hernici to defend themselves. The Aequi and Volsci, preferring plunder, turn away to Tusculum, and the people crowd the temples in prayer.","ideas":["A colony at Antium as a compromise over land","The decree that the consul should see that the state took no harm","Plague empties Rome of magistrates; aediles keep the watch","Rome saved by the enemy's taste for plunder"],"terms":[{"t":"colonia (colony)","d":"A settlement of citizens on conquered land, here proposed at Antium."},{"t":"iustitium (suspension of the courts)","d":"A halt to public business proclaimed in emergencies."}]},{"i":204,"j":212,"loc":"Book III 8–13","title":"Terentilius's law and the exile of Caeso","summary":"Recovered from the plague, Rome wins large victories over the Volsci and Aequi, and Lucretius triumphs. The tribune Terentilius Harsa proposes a board of five to write down limits on consular power, and the struggle over this law fills the following years amid prodigies and alleged wars. The young patrician Caeso Quinctius drives the tribunes from the Forum by force until the tribune Verginius prosecutes him on a capital charge. Despite his father Cincinnatus's pleas, the testimony of Volscius that Caeso killed his brother turns the people against him; Caeso is released on sureties, flees to Etruria, and his father is ruined paying the bail.","ideas":["Terentilius: consular power is a king's power in all but name","The patricians call the war news a ruse; tribunes call it a farce","Caeso Quinctius as the strong-armed champion of the young nobles","The first bail given to the people, forfeited by Caeso's flight"],"terms":[{"t":"rogatio Terentilia","d":"Terentilius's proposal to codify the laws governing consular authority."},{"t":"vades (sureties)","d":"Guarantors pledging money that an accused man would appear for trial."}]},{"i":213,"j":220,"loc":"Book III 14–21","title":"Herdonius seizes the Capitol; Cincinnatus as consul","summary":"The younger patricians block the law while courting the plebs, and the tribunes allege conspiracies. The Sabine Appius Herdonius seizes the Capitol by night with exiles and slaves, and the tribunes call it a sham to distract from the law. The consul Valerius rallies the people with an appeal to the captive gods, Tusculan troops arrive unasked, and the Capitol is retaken, though Valerius is killed. Cincinnatus, chosen consul in his place, rebukes both tribunes and senate, threatens to lead the army out under its oath and to have a dictator named, and finally refuses to be re-elected against the senate's resolution.","ideas":["Slaves and exiles holding Jupiter's temple","Tusculum's loyal help in retaking the Capitol","Valerius's promise to the plebs left unkept after his death","Cincinnatus will not imitate the tribunes by succeeding himself"],"terms":[{"t":"sacramentum (military oath)","d":"The oath binding soldiers to assemble at the consul's command, which Cincinnatus invokes."}]},{"i":221,"j":232,"loc":"Book III 22–30","title":"Cincinnatus called from the plough","summary":"Fabius takes the Volscian camp near Antium and helps the Tusculans recover their citadel from the Aequi. Volscius is exposed as a false witness against Caeso. When the Aequi under Cloelius Gracchus break the treaty and trap the consul Minucius on Algidus, the senate names Lucius Quinctius Cincinnatus dictator, and envoys find him working his four-acre farm. He musters the army overnight, surrounds the Aequi with a rampart, sends them under the yoke, triumphs, condemns Volscius, and resigns on the sixteenth day. Soon afterwards the number of tribunes is raised to ten.","ideas":["Virtue without wealth: the dictator found at the plough","The double encirclement of the Aequian camp on Algidus","The yoke of three spears as the sign of surrender","Ten tribunes elected, two from each class"],"terms":[{"t":"dictator","d":"A single magistrate appointed in crisis for up to six months, without appeal."},{"t":"magister equitum (master of the horse)","d":"The dictator's deputy, here Lucius Tarquitius."},{"t":"iugum (yoke)","d":"Two spears with a third across, under which the defeated passed in disgrace."}]},{"i":233,"j":241,"loc":"Book III 31–38","title":"The decemvirs and the Twelve Tables","summary":"After further wars and the fining of two consuls, the tribunes and patricians agree on a joint codification, and envoys are sent to Athens to copy Solon's laws. In the 302nd year of the City supreme power passes to ten men without appeal, led by Appius Claudius, who governs fairly and publishes ten tables of law for public comment. Appius then rigs the election of a second board, which appears with 120 lictors and axes, rules arbitrarily over the plebs, and adds two tables. When their term ends they keep their power without an election, and Sabine and Aequian invasions force them to summon the senate.","ideas":["The mission to Athens for Solon's laws","The first decemvirate's fairness and public review of the code","Appius's sudden courtship of the plebs to secure re-election","The second decemvirate as ten kings holding power past their term"],"terms":[{"t":"decemviri (the Ten)","d":"Ten magistrates without appeal, appointed to write the laws in place of consuls and tribunes."},{"t":"Twelve Tables","d":"The code of law the decemvirs drew up, which Livy calls the source of all public and private law."},{"t":"provocatio (appeal)","d":"A citizen's right to appeal to the people against a magistrate, suspended under the decemvirs."}]},{"i":242,"j":247,"loc":"Book III 39–43","title":"Defiance in the senate; the murder of Siccius","summary":"In the senate Lucius Valerius and Marcus Horatius denounce the decemvirs as ten Tarquins, and Gaius Claudius begs his nephew Appius to remember his country. Lucius Cornelius steers the senate toward fighting the wars first, and the levy is held. Under the decemvirs the armies are routed by the Sabines near Eretum and by the Aequi on Algidus. In the Sabine camp the outspoken veteran Lucius Siccius is sent out to choose a campsite and murdered by his escort, and the soldiers discover from the bodies that he was killed by his own men.","ideas":["Horatius: it was a king's pride, not the name, that Romans hated","The senators tolerate the decemvirs for fear of reviving the tribunate","Armies let themselves be beaten rather than win for the decemvirs","Siccius found facing his attackers, none of them enemies"],"terms":[{"t":"interrex","d":"A temporary patrician official appointed to hold elections when there were no regular magistrates."}]},{"i":248,"j":256,"loc":"Book III 44–51","title":"Verginia and the second secession begins","summary":"Appius Claudius, lusting after the plebeian girl Verginia, has his client Marcus Claudius claim her as his slave and, as judge, assigns her to the claimant pending her father's arrival. Her betrothed Icilius defies him, messengers fetch her father Verginius from the camp before Appius's order to detain him arrives, and on the next day Appius awards her to Marcus Claudius. Verginius takes her aside and stabs her with a butcher's knife, saying this is the only way to keep her free, and escapes to the army. Valerius and Horatius drive Appius from the Forum, and the soldiers march to Rome, seize the Aventine, and elect ten military tribunes.","ideas":["The false claim of slavery as a pretext for Appius's lust","Icilius: the decemvirs took appeal and tribunes but not wives' chastity","Verginius kills his daughter to assert her freedom","A parallel with Lucretia: the same cause ends the decemvirs as the kings"],"terms":[{"t":"vindiciae (interim custody)","d":"The ruling on who holds a disputed person until the case is decided, which Appius gives against liberty."},{"t":"tribuni militum (military tribunes)","d":"Ten officers the seceding soldiers choose to lead them."}]},{"i":257,"j":265,"loc":"Book III 51–55","title":"Secession, abdication, and the Valerio-Horatian laws","summary":"The Sabine army also secedes, and both armies join on the Aventine and then, on Duillius's advice, withdraw to the Sacred Mount, followed by the plebs. With Rome deserted the decemvirs agree to submit, and Valerius and Horatius negotiate: the plebs ask for the tribunate, the appeal, and amnesty, and demand the decemvirs be burned, which the envoys refuse. The decemvirs abdicate, the plebs return and elect tribunes including Verginius and Icilius, and Valerius and Horatius become consuls. They make plebiscites binding on the people, protect the appeal by law, restore the sacrosanctity of tribunes, and have senate decrees deposited with the aediles.","ideas":["Withdrawal to the Sacred Mount in imitation of the first secession","The envoys: the plebs need a shield more than a sword","Plebiscites made binding on the whole people","No magistrate may be created without appeal"],"terms":[{"t":"secessio plebis (secession)","d":"The plebs' withdrawal from the city to force concessions."},{"t":"plebiscitum","d":"A measure passed by the plebs voting by tribes, now made binding on all."},{"t":"sacrosanctus (inviolable)","d":"The religious protection of the tribunes, restored and backed by statute."}]},{"i":266,"j":271,"loc":"Book III 56–59","title":"The fate of Appius and the decemvirs","summary":"Verginius prosecutes Appius on the single charge of assigning a free person to a claimant, and orders him to prison unless he can prove his innocence before a referee. Appius, who abolished the appeal, now appeals to the people, reciting his services and the laws he drafted. The Twelve Tables are set up in bronze, and Gaius Claudius pleads for his nephew, but Appius kills himself in prison, as does Spurius Oppius after a scarred veteran testifies against him. The other decemvirs go into exile, Marcus Claudius is exiled to Tibur, and the tribune Duillius halts further prosecutions for the year.","ideas":["The man who denied appeal now invokes it","Verginia's spirit at rest once no guilty man remains","The Twelve Tables engraved on bronze","Duillius's restraint relieves the patricians' fear"],"terms":[]},{"i":272,"j":278,"loc":"Book III 60–63","title":"Victories of a free Rome; a popular triumph","summary":"Valerius waits until the Aequi and Volsci on Algidus scatter to plunder, then attacks and takes their camp, telling his men they fight for the first time as free men. Horatius, having restored his army's confidence with skirmishes, defeats the Sabines when his dismounted cavalry shore up a failing wing. The senate grants only one day of thanksgiving and refuses the consuls a triumph, alleging intimidation. Over Gaius Claudius's protest the tribune Icilius puts the question to the people, and for the first time a triumph is celebrated by the people's vote without the senate.","ideas":["Valerius: soldiers of a free people fight better than the decemvirs' armies","Cavalry fighting on foot shame the infantry into rallying","Triumph granted by the tribes against the senate's will"],"terms":[{"t":"supplicatio (thanksgiving)","d":"Days of public prayer for a victory, here limited by the senate to one."},{"t":"triumphus (triumph)","d":"The victory procession, normally granted by the senate."}]},{"i":279,"j":284,"loc":"Book III 64–68","title":"Duillius checks re-election; Quinctius's great speech","summary":"The tribunes plot to re-elect themselves and the consuls, but Duillius, presiding, refuses to accept their candidacies, gets the consuls to decline, and has the five tribunes elected co-opt the rest. Trebonius then passes a law requiring the election to continue until ten are chosen. When renewed strife lets the Aequi and Volsci raid up to the Esquiline Gate, the consul Titus Quinctius Capitolinus addresses the people. He lists the patricians' concessions, blames class discord for inviting the enemy, attacks the tribunes as flatterers of the plebs, and promises victory if they return to their old discipline.","ideas":["Duillius blocks the tribunes' self-perpetuation","Livy: men defending their liberty soon oppress others","Quinctius: discord is the poison of the city","Honest counsel against the flattery of popular leaders"],"terms":[{"t":"cooptatio (co-optation)","d":"Elected tribunes choosing their own colleagues to fill the board."}]},{"i":285,"j":291,"loc":"Book III 69–72; Summary of Book III","title":"Victory at Corbio and the shameful Ardea judgment","summary":"Quinctius's speech rouses the young men, the levy is held at once, and the army marches to Corbio. Agrippa Furius yields command to his colleague; Sulpicius's cavalry destroys the enemy horse, Agrippa hurls standards into the enemy to spur his men, and the camp is taken, though no triumph is sought. Asked to arbitrate a land dispute between Aricia and Ardea, the people listen to the aged Scaptius, who says the land once belonged to Corioli and so to Rome, and despite the consuls' and senate's pleas the tribes award it to themselves. The Summary of Book III follows.","ideas":["Command shared by courtesy between consuls","Agrippa throws standards into the enemy ranks","Scaptius's memory of the war at Corioli","A judge awarding the disputed land to itself disgraces Rome"],"terms":[{"t":"ager publicus (public land)","d":"Land owned by the Roman state, which the tribes vote the Ardea-Aricia territory to be."}]},{"i":292,"j":299,"loc":"Book IV, chs. 1–6","title":"Canuleius and the law of intermarriage","summary":"The tribune Gaius Canuleius proposes a law allowing patricians and plebeians to intermarry, while other tribunes propose that consuls may be chosen from either order. The consuls tell the senate that mixed marriages would pollute the gentes and confuse the auspices, and that plebeian consuls would mean the end of the patriciate. Canuleius answers that Rome has always taken in outsiders of merit, Numa, Tarquinius and Servius Tullius among them, and that new institutions are natural in a growing state. He says the plebs will not fight for masters who shut them out of office and marriage. When the consul Curtius says plebeians lack the auspices, the plebs grow angrier, and the patricians give way on intermarriage.","ideas":["Patricians see intermarriage as defiling their blood and auspices","Canuleius cites foreign-born kings to argue that merit, not birth, should count","Rome has always added new offices and rights","Intermarriage is conceded in the hope of stopping the demand for plebeian consuls"],"terms":[{"t":"conubium (intermarriage)","d":"The legal right of marriage between the two orders, which the decemvirs had forbidden and which Canuleius's law restores."},{"t":"auspicia (auspices)","d":"The right to take omens on behalf of the state, which the patricians claim belongs to them alone."}]},{"i":300,"j":309,"loc":"Book IV, chs. 6–12","title":"Consular tribunes, the censorship, and Ardea","summary":"As a compromise, military tribunes with consular authority may now be chosen from either order, yet the people elect only patricians, and Livy praises their restraint. The first tribunes resign over a flaw in the auspices, and consuls replace them; Livy weighs the evidence of the Linen Rolls for their names. The censorship is created to take the census, a minor office that Livy says later gained great power over morals and rank. At Ardea a quarrel over a plebeian girl's marriage turns into civil war; the consul Geganius traps the Volscian besiegers and sends them under the yoke. Rome later gives Ardea's land back by enrolling Rutulian colonists, and Quinctius keeps peace at home.","ideas":["Consular tribunes created, but the voters choose only patricians","The censorship begins as a clerical task","A dispute over one marriage tears Ardea apart","Rome quietly undoes its unjust judgment against Ardea"],"terms":[{"t":"tribuni militum consulari potestate (military tribunes with consular power)","d":"A board of magistrates, open to plebeians, that sometimes replaced the two consuls."},{"t":"censores (censors)","d":"Magistrates created to take the census, who later came to regulate rank, morals and public revenues."}]},{"i":310,"j":314,"loc":"Book IV, chs. 12–16","title":"Famine and the killing of Spurius Maelius","summary":"A severe famine strikes Rome, and Lucius Minucius, prefect of the corn-supply, can do little but share out the shortage. Spurius Maelius, a rich knight, buys grain in Etruria and gives it away free; the crowds that follow him lead him to aim at kingship. Minucius reports arms and meetings at his house, and the consul Quinctius has the aged Cincinnatus named dictator, with Servilius Ahala as master of the horse. When Maelius refuses the summons and appeals to the crowd, Ahala kills him, and Cincinnatus tells the assembly the killing was just. Maelius's house is torn down, leaving the site called Aequimaelium; Minucius is honoured, and Livy rejects the story that he became an eleventh tribune.","ideas":["Private generosity used to buy power","Cincinnatus, past eighty, made dictator to meet the threat","Ahala kills Maelius for refusing the dictator's summons","The empty site of the house is kept as a warning"],"terms":[{"t":"praefectus annonae (prefect of the corn-supply)","d":"An official appointed to manage the grain supply during the famine."},{"t":"Aequimaelium","d":"The bare site of Maelius's demolished house, kept as a reminder of his punishment."}]},{"i":315,"j":324,"loc":"Book IV, chs. 17–22","title":"Fidenae's revolt and the spoils of Cossus","summary":"Fidenae goes over to Lars Tolumnius of Veii and kills four Roman envoys at his command; Livy rejects the excuse that the order was a remark made over dice. The dictator Mamercus Aemilius defeats Veii, Fidenae and the Faliscans, and the cavalry officer Aulus Cornelius Cossus kills Tolumnius in single combat. Cossus dedicates the second spolia opima in the temple of Jupiter Feretrius. Livy notes that the inscription, which Augustus himself read, calls Cossus consul, and he discusses the problem this makes for the annals. Plague follows, Fidenae and Veii march up to the Colline Gate, and the dictator Servilius takes Fidenae by digging a tunnel into its citadel.","ideas":["The killing of envoys violates the law of nations","Cossus kills a king and wins the spoils of honour","Livy weighs the inscription and Augustus's testimony against the annals","Fidenae is captured through a mine under its citadel"],"terms":[{"t":"spolia opima (spoils of honour)","d":"Armour stripped by a Roman commander from an enemy commander he killed himself; Cossus's are the second after Romulus's."},{"t":"ius gentium (law of nations)","d":"The shared rules between peoples, including the safety of envoys, which the Fidenates broke."}]},{"i":325,"j":336,"loc":"Book IV, chs. 23–29","title":"Mamercus limits the censorship; victory on Algidus","summary":"Livy notes conflicting sources for the year's magistrates. When an all-Etruscan war fails to come, the dictator Mamercus Aemilius passes a law cutting the censors' term from five years to eighteen months, and the angry censors punish him. Plague and famine follow; the tribunes ban whitened togas to stop canvassing, and the senate answers by holding consular elections. With the Aequi and Volsci on Algidus and the consuls quarrelling, the tribunes force the consuls to name a dictator, Aulus Postumius Tubertus. He wins a hard night-and-dawn battle despite the Volscian Vettius Messius, takes both camps and triumphs. Livy doubts the story that he beheaded his own son, and he notes the Carthaginians' first army in Sicily.","ideas":["Mamercus argues that great powers must be limited in time","The tribunes' threat of prison makes the consuls name a dictator","Postumius's combined attack wins on Algidus","Livy rejects the tale that Postumius executed his son"],"terms":[{"t":"dictator","d":"A single magistrate with supreme authority, named by a consul in a crisis and holding office for a short time."}]},{"i":337,"j":344,"loc":"Book IV, chs. 30–34","title":"Drought, foreign rites, and Fidenae falls again","summary":"A truce is granted to the Aequi, and a drought brings disease to cattle and people; foreign rites spread until the aediles are told to allow only Roman gods worshipped in the ancestral way. Fetials demand redress from Veii, and the tribunes make sure the people vote the war. Three consular tribunes give conflicting orders and are beaten at Veii, so Mamercus Aemilius is named dictator a third time, with Cossus as master of the horse. Fidenae kills its new settlers and joins Veii; Mamercus tells the frightened citizens that the enemy is the same one they have often beaten. At Fidenae the townsmen charge with torches, Cossus leads unbridled horses through the flames, and the city is stormed. Livy doubts reports of a river battle.","ideas":["Only Roman gods, worshipped in the Roman way, are to be allowed","Divided command loses a battle","The torch attack is turned back on the enemy","Fidenae is taken and Mamercus resigns within sixteen days"],"terms":[{"t":"fetiales (fetials)","d":"Priests who formally demanded redress from another people before war could be declared."}]},{"i":345,"j":354,"loc":"Book IV, chs. 35–42","title":"Sempronius's battle and Tempanius's cavalry","summary":"After truces and games, the plebeian tribunes reproach the plebs for never electing their own men. The consular tribunes slip a vote for consular elections past them, and the Samnites seize Capua. The consul Sempronius fights the Volsci carelessly, and the decurion Sextus Tempanius dismounts the cavalry and holds the line. Cut off on a hillock, his men hold out until both armies, unsure who has won, abandon their camps by night. Back in Rome, Tempanius declines to blame his general and is praised for his modesty. Postumius is fined for the defeat at Veii, Quinctius is acquitted, and the tribune Hortensius drops his prosecution of Sempronius when four colleagues plead for their old commander.","ideas":["The plebs fail to elect plebeians even when the law allows it","Tempanius's dismounted horsemen save the battle","A soldier declines to judge his general","Hortensius yields to his colleagues' loyalty"],"terms":[{"t":"decurio (decurion)","d":"An officer commanding a small squadron of cavalry, the rank Tempanius holds."}]},{"i":355,"j":364,"loc":"Book IV, chs. 43–47","title":"Quaestors, a slave plot, and Labici","summary":"A plan to double the quaestors leads to a fight over whether plebeians may hold the office, and the year passes in interregna until Papirius proposes a compromise. The people still elect only patricians, and the angry tribunes prosecute Gaius Sempronius, who refuses to back a land law and is fined. The Vestal Postumia is tried for unchastity and acquitted, but told to dress more modestly. Two informers reveal a slave plot to burn the City, and they are rewarded with freedom. When the Labicani join the Aequi, two quarrelling consular tribunes are routed; the elder Servilius Priscus is named dictator, takes the Aequian camp and Labici, and a colony is planted there.","ideas":["Plebeians gain the right to stand for quaestor but are not elected","Sempronius puts the public good above his own defence","A slave conspiracy is betrayed and crushed","Rivalry between colleagues causes defeat; a dictator restores matters"],"terms":[{"t":"quaestores (quaestors)","d":"Financial officials, increased to four, two of them to serve the consuls in war."},{"t":"interrex","d":"A patrician who held office for five days at a time when there were no chief magistrates, in order to hold elections."}]},{"i":365,"j":371,"loc":"Book IV, chs. 48–52","title":"Agrarian laws, Bolae, and Postumius's murder","summary":"Two tribunes propose dividing all conquered land, which would strip much of the nobility. The young Appius Claudius revives his family's tactic of winning over other tribunes to veto it, and six do so. Bolae is taken and retaken; the consular tribune Marcus Postumius breaks his promise of booty to his soldiers and then threatens them in public. The tribune Sextius uses the remark against him, and when Postumius punishes mutineers savagely, his own soldiers stone him to death. The consuls handle the inquiry mildly, but the plebs resent that the land law is ignored while punishment is swift. Pestilence and then famine follow, and corn is bought from Etruria and Sicily.","ideas":["The veto of other tribunes used against tribunes' own laws","Postumius's broken promise and threat turn the army against him","A Roman commander killed by his own troops","Plague and famine pause the political strife"],"terms":[{"t":"intercessio (veto)","d":"A tribune's power to block an act, here used by some tribunes against their colleagues."},{"t":"ager publicus (public domain)","d":"State land taken in war, much of it held by nobles, which agrarian laws sought to divide."}]},{"i":372,"j":377,"loc":"Book IV, chs. 53–57","title":"Carventum and the first plebeian quaestors","summary":"The tribune Menenius blocks the levy over the land question until the loss of the citadel of Carventum lets his colleagues authorise the consul to force men to serve. The consul Valerius recaptures it but sells the spoils for the treasury, and his soldiers sing verses praising Menenius at his ovation. The angry plebs elect three plebeian quaestors for the first time, encouraged by the Icilii, and the patricians fear their offices will be lost. The Icilii force elections of consular tribunes, but the patricians win them all, said to be by putting up unworthy plebeian candidates. When the senate wants a dictator against the Volsci at Antium, Servilius Ahala overrides his colleagues and names Publius Cornelius.","ideas":["Military setbacks are used on both sides of the political fight","Plebeians win the quaestorship for the first time","The patricians manage the field of candidates","Servilius Ahala puts the state above his colleagues"],"terms":[]},{"i":378,"j":387,"loc":"Book IV, chs. 58–61; Summary of Book IV","title":"Pay for the army and the siege of Veii","summary":"The garrison at Verrugo falls because help arrives too late, and Veii answers Roman envoys with a threat. The plebeian tribunes urge the young men to refuse the new war. The three consular tribunes ravage Volscian lands and Fabius takes Anxur, sharing its riches with his colleagues' armies. The senate then votes, without being asked, that soldiers be paid from the treasury, and the plebs rejoice. The tribunes object that the war-tax will fall on the people, but senators and leading plebeians pay first and the rest follow. The siege of Veii begins, Artena is taken through a slave's betrayal, and a summary of Book IV follows.","ideas":["Pay for soldiers is granted freely by the senate","Senators set the example by paying the war-tax first","The siege of Veii begins","Artena falls through betrayal"],"terms":[{"t":"stipendium (military pay)","d":"Pay for soldiers from the public treasury, granted here for the first time."},{"t":"tributum (war-tax)","d":"A property tax raised from the citizens to fund the soldiers' pay."}]},{"i":388,"j":398,"loc":"Book V, chs. 1–6","title":"Winter siege and Appius Claudius's speech","summary":"Rome elects eight consular tribunes, while Veii chooses a king whom the Etruscans hate for disrupting a sacred festival, so they refuse to help Veii. The Romans build double siege-works and winter quarters, which the plebeian tribunes call a permanent slavery for the soldiers. Appius Claudius replies that the tribunes fear harmony between the orders, that pay brings a duty of year-round service, and that abandoning the works would invite attack and Etruscan aid. He argues that perseverance is the key to sieges, that Rome's reputation depends on it, and that tribunician licence has turned liberty into disregard for senate, laws and discipline.","ideas":["The Etruscans refuse to aid Veii because of its king","Year-round service is a new burden on Roman soldiers","Appius: pay and duty go together","Persistence wins sieges and sets Rome's reputation"],"terms":[{"t":"hiberna (winter quarters)","d":"Shelters for an army to stay in the field through winter, built at Veii for the first time."}]},{"i":399,"j":406,"loc":"Book V, chs. 7–12","title":"Volunteer horsemen and the quarrel of Sergius and Verginius","summary":"The Veientes burn the Roman siege-works in a night sortie. In response, men of equestrian rating offer to serve on their own horses, the plebs volunteer for service at Veii, and the senate rewards both with pay. Anxur is lost through neglect. When the Capenates and Faliscans attack, Verginius will not help Sergius, his personal enemy, and Sergius would rather be beaten than ask, so his camp is lost. The senate removes the year's magistrates early, and Servilius Ahala threatens to name a dictator if they refuse to resign. Amid levies and taxes, tribunes are co-opted against the Trebonian law, and Sergius and Verginius are tried and fined.","ideas":["A disaster unites the orders and brings volunteers","Cavalry begin serving on their own horses","Private hatred between commanders loses a camp","The failed generals are made scapegoats for the people's anger"],"terms":[{"t":"Trebonian law","d":"A law forbidding the tribunes of the plebs to fill vacancies in their college by co-opting colleagues."}]},{"i":407,"j":418,"loc":"Book V, chs. 12–18","title":"First plebeian consular tribune and the Alban Lake","summary":"Publius Licinius Calvus becomes the first plebeian consular tribune, and the next year most of those elected are plebeians. A harsh winter and a plague lead to the first lectisternium, with open houses and prisoners freed. The Romans defeat a second relief attack at Veii. The patricians use religious scruples to win back the elections. A seer from Veii, seized by trickery, declares that Veii cannot fall until the Alban Lake is drained, and the Delphic oracle confirms it, adding that neglected rites must be renewed. Faulty elections are blamed, an interregnum is held, and Licinius has his son elected in his place, while Genucius falls in an ambush that sets off panic.","ideas":["Plebeians reach the consular tribunate","The lectisternium feasts the gods during a plague","The Alban Lake prophecy links Veii's fate to a ritual act","Flawed auspices must be corrected before victory"],"terms":[{"t":"lectisternium","d":"A rite in which images of the gods were placed on couches and offered a feast, held in Rome for the first time here."},{"t":"Sibylline Books","d":"The prophetic books consulted by the duumvirs in times of crisis."}]},{"i":419,"j":427,"loc":"Book V, chs. 19–23","title":"Camillus takes Veii","summary":"Marcus Furius Camillus is named dictator, restores discipline, defeats the Faliscans and Capenates, and drives a mine into Veii's citadel. He asks the senate how to divide the booty; on Licinius's advice, anyone who wishes may go to the camp to share it. Camillus vows a tithe to Apollo and invites Juno of Veii to Rome; his men burst from the mine into her temple and the city is taken. Livy dismisses the tale of the stolen entrails as stage material. Camillus prays that any envy of Rome's fortune fall lightly, and his stumble is later read as an omen. Juno is said to nod assent before being taken to the Aventine, and Camillus's white-horse triumph and the tithe offend the people.","ideas":["Veii falls after a ten-year siege, by mine rather than assault","Evocatio: Juno is called to move to Rome","Camillus fears divine envy of Rome's success","The triumph and the tithe make Camillus unpopular"],"terms":[{"t":"evocatio","d":"A rite calling an enemy city's god to leave it and accept a new temple in Rome, as Camillus does with Juno."}]},{"i":428,"j":438,"loc":"Book V, chs. 24–28","title":"The tithe, Falerii, and the schoolmaster","summary":"The plebs reject a small colony and propose that half the people move to Veii, but the patricians fiercely oppose it. Camillus insists the tithe covers the captured city and land; the matrons give their gold, and a golden bowl is made for Delphi. In the Faliscan war a schoolmaster leads the sons of Falerii's leading men into the Roman camp. Camillus refuses the offer, saying Rome does not make war on children, and has the boys whip the traitor home. Moved by his justice, the Faliscans surrender. Liparaean pirates capture the envoys taking the bowl, but their chief magistrate Timasitheus sends them on safely, and Postumius makes up for a rout by destroying the Aequian army.","ideas":["The plan to move to Veii first appears","The matrons give their gold for Apollo's offering","Camillus's justice wins Falerii without a battle","Timasitheus's reverence protects the Delphic offering"],"terms":[{"t":"decuma (tithe)","d":"The tenth of the spoils of Veii vowed to Pythian Apollo."}]},{"i":439,"j":446,"loc":"Book V, chs. 29–32","title":"Veii bill defeated; Camillus goes into exile","summary":"The plebs fine two former tribunes for having vetoed the Veii bill, and Camillus warns that this destroys the veto. He urges the senators to oppose the bill as men fighting for their temples and native soil. The senators plead with their tribesmen in the Forum, and the bill fails by one tribe; each plebeian is then given seven iugera of Veientine land. Consuls return, campaigns are fought against the Aequi, Volsinii and Sappinum, and a censor is replaced after his death, which is later thought to have offended the gods. A voice in the Nova Via warns that the Gauls are coming but is ignored, and Camillus, prosecuted over the spoils of Veii, goes into exile.","ideas":["Pleading by the patricians, not force, defeats the migration","Land at Veii is given out to win over the plebs","The divine warning is ignored because the man who heard it was humble","Rome loses Camillus just before its greatest danger"],"terms":[{"t":"iugerum","d":"A Roman unit of land area; seven iugera are given to each free plebeian."}]},{"i":447,"j":454,"loc":"Book V, chs. 33–37","title":"The Gauls come; the Fabii break the law of nations","summary":"Envoys from Clusium ask for help against the Gauls. Livy gives the tale of Arruns luring them with wine, then the wider history: Etruscan power across Italy, and the migrations under Bellovesus and others that founded Mediolanium and settled the Po valley, with the Senones the last to arrive. Three sons of Fabius Ambustus are sent as envoys, but they fight in the Etruscan line, and Quintus Fabius kills a Gallic chief. The Gauls demand the Fabii be handed over; the senate refers it to the people, who instead elect the Fabii consular tribunes. The enraged Gauls march straight on Rome, and a hastily raised army meets them at the Allia.","ideas":["Gallic migration into Italy, with the Etruscan background","The Gauls claim right by the sword","Roman envoys violate the law of nations","Rome names no dictator against an unknown enemy"],"terms":[{"t":"ius gentium (law of nations)","d":"The rule that envoys must not take part in fighting, which the Fabii break."}]},{"i":455,"j":458,"loc":"Book V, chs. 38–40","title":"Rout on the Allia and the abandonment of Rome","summary":"The tribunes form a thin line at the Allia without a camp or the auspices. Brennus attacks the reserves on the hill, and the rest of the army flees without fighting, most of them to Veii and the rest to the Citadel at Rome. The surprised Gauls camp outside the open city. The Romans decide that the men of fighting age and the senators will hold the Capitol and Citadel, while the old men who have held high office stay behind to die. Many plebeians scatter toward Janiculum. The flamen of Quirinus and the Vestals bury some sacred objects and carry the rest; the plebeian Lucius Albinius puts his family off his wagon to carry them to Caere.","ideas":["The army at the Allia breaks almost without a blow","The Capitol is held as the last refuge of gods and men","The old men choose to stay and die","Albinius puts the sacred objects before his family's comfort"],"terms":[{"t":"flamen Quirinalis","d":"The priest of Quirinus, who with the Vestals rescues the sacred objects of the state."}]},{"i":459,"j":464,"loc":"Book V, chs. 41–44","title":"The sack of Rome and Camillus at Ardea","summary":"The old nobles sit in their robes of office on ivory chairs, and the Gauls stare at them in awe as if at gods. When a Gaul strokes Marcus Papirius's beard, Papirius strikes him and is killed, and the massacre and burning follow. From the Citadel the Romans watch their City destroyed but resolve to hold the hill. The Gauls' assault up the slope is thrown back, so they settle into a blockade and send foragers out. At Ardea the exiled Camillus addresses the council: the Gauls bring more terror than strength and sleep unguarded, and he promises to deliver them to the Ardeates asleep.","ideas":["The seated elders and Papirius's blow","The defenders watch the City burn from the Citadel","A failed assault turns into a blockade","Camillus offers Ardea a night attack"],"terms":[{"t":"sella curulis (curule chair)","d":"The ivory seat of high magistrates, on which the old men wait for death."}]},{"i":465,"j":471,"loc":"Book V, chs. 45–49","title":"Geese on the Capitol and the gold ransom","summary":"Camillus leads the Ardeates against the sleeping Gauls and slaughters them, and the Romans at Veii under the centurion Caedicius do the same to raiding Etruscans. On the Capitol, Gaius Fabius Dorsuo walks through the enemy lines to perform his clan's sacrifice on the Quirinal and returns unharmed. Pontius Cominus floats down the Tiber and climbs to the Capitol, and Camillus is recalled and named dictator. The Gauls climb the cliff by night, but Juno's sacred geese wake Marcus Manlius, who throws them down. Famine forces the garrison to agree a ransom of a thousand pounds of gold; Brennus adds his sword to false weights, but Camillus arrives, voids the deal and destroys the Gauls.","ideas":["The night massacres at Ardea and near Veii","Fabius Dorsuo keeps the rites under siege","Manlius and the geese save the Capitol","Brennus's sword on the scales; Camillus wins Rome back with iron"],"terms":[{"t":"vae victis (woe to the conquered)","d":"Brennus's taunt as he throws his sword onto the scales for the ransom."},{"t":"lex curiata (curiate law)","d":"The vote of the curiate assembly that recalls Camillus and confirms him as dictator."}]},{"i":472,"j":481,"loc":"Book V, chs. 49–55; Summary of Book V","title":"Camillus's speech against leaving Rome","summary":"Hailed as a second founder, Camillus remains dictator. He has the shrines purified, makes a pact of hospitality with Caere, sets up Capitoline Games and a temple to Aius Locutius, and deposits the gold under Jupiter's throne. When the tribunes again urge a move to Veii, he argues that Rome prospered when it obeyed the gods and suffered when it neglected them. He says its rites, priests and assemblies are bound to the City's sacred ground, and that leaving now would be a defeat that glorifies the Gauls. He praises the site itself, its hills, river and central place in Italy. A centurion's chance words in the Forum are taken as an omen, the bill fails, and the City is rebuilt in haste and disorder. A summary of Book V follows.","ideas":["Religious restoration comes first after the victory","Obeying the gods brings success, neglecting them disaster","Rome's rites cannot be moved to Veii","A centurion's chance words settle the matter; hasty rebuilding leaves streets irregular"],"terms":[{"t":"Aius Locutius","d":"The name under which the voice that warned of the Gauls was honoured with a temple in the Nova Via."},{"t":"pomerium","d":"The sacred boundary of the City, within which the auspices and assemblies had to be held."}]}]});